# Phase 16 — Docker, CI/CD, Cloud Deployment and Reproducible Builds

## Status

Completed.

## Objective

Build a production-style delivery pipeline for DecisionAI that can package the API and UI into containers, validate every change automatically, deploy immutable application versions, authenticate GitHub Actions to AWS without static credentials, publish images to Amazon ECR, register and update Amazon ECS task definitions, preserve low-cost shutdown behavior, and reproduce dependency versions consistently across local, CI, Docker, and cloud environments.

## Architecture Overview

```text
Developer
   |
   v
git push main
   |
   v
GitHub Actions CI
   |
   +--> install locked dev dependencies
   +--> Ruff
   +--> pytest
   +--> build API image
   +--> build UI image
   |
   v
GitHub Actions Deploy
   |
   +--> GitHub OIDC
   +--> temporary AWS credentials
   +--> build images from production lock
   +--> tag images with tested Git SHA
   +--> push to Amazon ECR
   +--> register ECS task definitions
   +--> update ECS services
   |
   v
Amazon ECS Fargate
```

The API and UI are deployed as separate ECS services.

## Containerization

DecisionAI uses:

```text
Dockerfile.api
Dockerfile.ui
```

The API container runs FastAPI through Uvicorn and exposes port 8000.

Runtime command:

```text
python -m uvicorn app.api.main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port ${PORT:-8000}
```

The UI container runs Streamlit and exposes port 8501.

Runtime command:

```text
python -m streamlit run app/ui/streamlit_app.py   --server.address 0.0.0.0   --server.port ${PORT:-8501}
```

## Immutable Base Image

The Python base image is pinned by digest:

```text
python:3.14-slim@sha256:c3e521df8b2b498a7a682e7e18676771cb80c6b75b8699af886b2d554ce40151
```

This makes the base runtime image explicit rather than relying only on a mutable tag.

## Dependency Reproducibility Problem

The project originally declared direct dependencies in `pyproject.toml` without exact versions.

That caused dependency drift: the same source commit could resolve different package versions locally and in GitHub builds.

Observed examples included differences in:

```text
google-genai
openai
fastapi
streamlit
uvicorn
pandas
```

This showed that source control alone was not enough to guarantee a reproducible runtime.

## Chosen Locking Strategy

The project adopted `pip-tools`.

```text
pyproject.toml
     |
     v
pip-compile
     |
     +--> requirements.lock
     |
     +--> requirements-dev.lock
```

`requirements.lock` contains exact production dependency versions.

`requirements-dev.lock` contains production dependencies plus development tooling such as `pytest` and `ruff`.

